    "teacher_markdown": "### **Teacher Tips**\r\n\r\n**Students may think a named function changes what the code does**\r\n\r\nSome students may focus on the new syntax and assume the behavior should change. Emphasize that refactoring changes how the code is organized, not what it does. Ask: **\"Before and after the refactor, what happens when you click Skip 10s?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n\r\nBefore:\r\n```javascript\r\nforwardButton.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\r\n  progress = progress + 10;\r\n  progressBar.style.width = progress + \"%\";\r\n  darkOverlay.style.opacity = (100 - progress) / 100;\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nAfter:\r\n```javascript\r\nconst skip = () => {\r\n  progress = progress + 10;\r\n  progressBar.style.width = progress + \"%\";\r\n  darkOverlay.style.opacity = (100 - progress) / 100;\r\n};\r\n\r\nforwardButton.addEventListener(\"click\", skip);\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may confuse the function definition with the function call**\r\n\r\nWhen connecting the event listener, students often write `skip()` instead of `skip`. Help them understand that the event listener needs a function reference, not an immediate function call.\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nforwardButton.addEventListener(\"click\", skip);\r\n```\r\n\r\nAsk: **\"Are we telling JavaScript what function to run later, or are we running it right now?\"**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may copy the Skip function but forget to change the logic**\r\n\r\nWhen creating `rewind`, some students will successfully create the function but leave the line that increases progress. Encourage them to compare the intended behavior to the code by asking: **\"If the progress bar should move backward, what should happen to the value of `progress`?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nconst rewind = () => {\r\n  progress = progress - 10;\r\n  progressBar.style.width = progress + \"%\";\r\n  darkOverlay.style.opacity = (100 - progress) / 100;\r\n};\r\n```"
